[line]

[size=5][b]Your base in 3D[/b][/size]

Open a region save and choose the [b]Bases[/b] tab. The [b]Map[/b] shows your bases on a top-down picture of the level. The [b]3D view[/b] draws them with the game's own models and the level around them.

[list]
[*][b]Look around:[/b] drag to turn, scroll to zoom, or press [b]Walk[/b] to explore at eye height.
[*][b]Click anything:[/b] a crate shows its contents, a bench its upgrades, a door opens or closes, a trader shows what they sell, a containment cell shows its creature.
[*][b]Change your base:[/b] turn on [b]Edit mode[/b], then drag pieces around, turn them, copy them or remove them. The view warns you when a piece cuts into a wall or floats in the air.
[*][b]Add new pieces:[/b] [b]Add object[/b] opens a palette of pictures. Drag one onto the view to place it, even kinds you have only built in your other worlds.
[*][b]Wire up power:[/b] plug devices into outlets and run cables through cable reroutes, the way the game does.
[*][b]Show in 3D:[/b] most lists have a button that takes you straight to that door, crate, button or character.
[/list]

The models are read from your own installed game. The first time, the editor needs a few minutes to read the world: the start page offers to [b]get the 3D view ready[/b] once, in the background, so every area opens quickly afterwards. It offers again after a game update. The browser version draws every piece as a box.

[center][img]https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/screenshots/52-3d-add-object.png[/img]
[i]Add object: drag a picture onto the view to place it.[/i]

[img]https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/screenshots/51-3d-show-menu.png[/img]
[i]The Show menu: labels, markers, filters and the level around your base.[/i]

[img]https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/screenshots/54-world-map.png[/img]
[i]The Bases map, over a top-down picture of the level.[/i]

[img]https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/screenshots/53-prepare-3d.png[/img]
[i]Get the 3D view ready once, and every area opens quickly.[/i][/center]

3D guide: https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/guide/3d-view

[line]

[size=5][b]Is it safe?[/b][/size]

Edits wait until you press SAVE, and every save keeps a backup copy of the old file right next to it. The editor writes the save in the game's exact format, so anything you do not touch stays exactly as the game wrote it. It offers to set a quest's requirements for you instead of letting you skip ahead into a broken story.

It is still a save editor. Please keep your own copy of any world you really care about.

[b]Live editing is the exception:[/b] it changes a running game straight away, with no SAVE, no undo and no editor backup. Make your own in-game backup before using it.

[line]

[size=5][b]Works with Steam AND Game Pass[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Steam:[/b] your normal save folder. The editor usually finds it on its own.
[*][b]Game Pass / Microsoft Store:[/b] these saves are packed into Xbox containers with no loose [b].sav[/b] files. The editor reads, edits and writes them anyway, and lists them with a [b]GAME PASS[/b] tag.
[*][b]Dedicated servers:[/b] point it at the folder with your Worlds and edit any player or the world itself.
[*][b]Switching platforms:[/b] convert a world from Game Pass to Steam or back in a couple of clicks. Difficulty settings come along, and a character can be handed to a different account at the same time.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Game Pass: if your save stopped loading, or your edits keep vanishing[/b][/size]

[b]1. If a world will not load, or edits keep coming back undone:[/b] close the game and the Xbox app, open the Game Pass world in the editor, and choose [b]Repair now[/b] if it offers. Your whole save folder is copied to a backup first. (Older versions before v2.5 could leave a Game Pass save in this state; that is fixed.)

[b]2. Then edit offline, every time.[/b] Xbox keeps a copy of your save in the cloud, and when the two disagree the cloud copy can win.

[list=1]
[*]Close Abiotic Factor [b]and[/b] the Xbox app (check the system tray).
[*]Wait about a minute for the last upload to finish.
[*]Go offline: Wi-Fi off, or airplane mode.
[*]Make your edits and press [b]SAVE[/b].
[*][b]Still offline[/b], launch the game, load the world, save in game, and quit. This step makes the game adopt your edit as the newest copy.
[*]Only now reconnect. If Xbox asks which copy to keep, pick the one from [b]this device[/b].
[/list]

[b]Careful with that prompt:[/b] the copy you do not pick is deleted, and Xbox does not show you either one first. If you would rather avoid all of this, convert your world to Steam and edit that instead.

[b]3. If a world has disappeared, it is probably still there.[/b] Xbox usually stops listing a world rather than deleting it. The editor can put it back on the list, or restore the spare copy the game keeps one save behind. Your save folder is backed up first.

The editor also refuses to save while the game is running or while Xbox has an unsettled conflict, and tells you what to do instead.

Full walkthrough: https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/guide/game-pass

[line]

[size=5][b]Live editing: change a world while it is running[/b][/size]

The desktop app can connect to a world that is loaded in the game right now, so you do not need to close Abiotic Factor to make a change.

[list]
[*][b]Desktop app only,[/b] on Windows, and on Linux when the game runs through Steam Play (Proton; you also need Wine). Works with a local game or a dedicated server you run.
[*][b]No SAVE button, no undo, no backup.[/b] Changes apply the moment you make them. Make your own in-game backup first.
[*][b]Needs UE4SS,[/b] a separate open-source mod loader. The release includes a copy and installs it into your game folder with your permission. An existing UE4SS install is left alone.
[*][b]What you can change:[/b] vitals, skills, inventories, transmog, recipes, GatePal records, traits, looks, spawn point, story flags and chapters, clock and weather, containers, ground items, creatures and story characters, traders, pets, doors, portals, elevators, buttons, resource nodes, breakable objects, corpses, NPC spawners, trams, vehicles, containment units, bases and bench upgrades, garden plots, Power Chairs and more. Some changes need you to be the host; the guide lists every one.
[/list]

To try it: open the editing-mode button in the app, choose [b]Set up live editing[/b], then [b]This PC[/b], and follow the steps.

Live editing guide: https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/guide/live-editing

[line]

[size=5][b]How do I use it?[/b][/size]

[list=1]
[*][b]Close the game[/b] and copy your world folder somewhere safe.
[*][b]Open the editor[/b] and choose [b]Open offline editor[/b]. It usually finds your saves on its own (Steam and Game Pass), so you just click OPEN.
[*][b]Pick a player or area and edit it.[/b] Drag a slider, tick a recipe, drop in an item. Nothing is written yet.
[*][b]Press SAVE.[/b] Your changes are written, and a backup copy of the old save is kept right next to it.
[/list]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installing on Windows[/b][/size]

Download the Windows file from the FILES tab, unzip it anywhere, and run [b]AbioticEditor.Web.exe[/b]. It opens in its own window and needs nothing else installed. The 3D view comes with it.

[b]About the "unknown publisher" warning:[/b] this free fan-made tool is not signed with a paid certificate, so Windows SmartScreen (and sometimes antivirus) may warn you the first time you run it.

To get past it: right-click the downloaded zip, choose [b]Properties[/b], tick [b]Unblock[/b], click [b]OK[/b], then unzip and run. If SmartScreen still shows "Windows protected your PC", click [b]More info[/b] then [b]Run anyway[/b].

Prefer no warning at all? Install it with Scoop, a Windows package manager:

[code]scoop bucket add abiotic-editor https://github.com/ChristopherVR/AbioticEditor
scoop install abiotic-editor[/code]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installing on Linux / Steam Deck[/b][/size]

On Steam Deck, switch to [b]Desktop Mode[/b] first: hold the Power button and choose [b]Switch to Desktop[/b].

Download the Linux / Steam Deck file from the FILES tab, then:

[list=1]
[*]Right-click the downloaded zip and choose [b]Extract[/b]. (Running a file from inside the zip does not work.)
[*]Open the extracted folder and double-click [b]launch-linux.desktop[/b].
[*]Choose [b]Trust and Launch[/b].
[/list]

The editor opens in its own window and finds saves inside Steam Play / Proton folders automatically.

[size=4][b]If double-clicking launch-linux.desktop does nothing[/b][/size]

Right-click empty space inside the extracted folder, choose [b]Open Terminal Here[/b], then run:

[code]bash launch-linux.sh[/code]

Then run [code]bash install-linux-desktop.sh[/code] once to add the editor to your app menu.

If the terminal reports missing GTK 3, WebKitGTK 4.1 or libnotify libraries on Steam Deck, use the built-in fallback instead, which needs none of them:

[code]./launch-linux.sh --headless[/code]

Then open [b]http://127.0.0.1:37246[/b] in a web browser on the Deck. The editor works the same way, inside a browser tab.

On a regular Linux desktop, install the libraries instead, for example on Ubuntu 24.04:

[code]sudo apt-get install libgtk-3-0t64 libwebkit2gtk-4.1-0 libnotify4[/code]

[line]

[size=5][b]Installing on macOS[/b][/size]

Download the macOS file from GitHub Releases: [b]osx-arm64[/b] for Apple silicon Macs (M1 or later), [b]osx-x64[/b] for older Intel Macs. Unzip it, open [b]Terminal[/b] in that folder, and run:

[code]bash launch-mac.sh[/code]

Live editing is not available on macOS. Everything else works.

[line]

[size=5][b]No download needed: it runs in your browser too[/b][/size]

The editor also runs as a web page: [url=https://christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/app/]christophervr.github.io/AbioticEditor/app[/url]

Press [b]OPEN FOLDER[/b], choose your save folder, and edit. Nothing is uploaded: your saves are read straight off your own disk.

[list]
[*][b]Chrome or Edge:[/b] your changes are written back to the folder, with a backup copy, like the app.
[*][b]Firefox or Safari:[/b] these browsers cannot write to your disk, so press [b]EXPORT[/b] to download your changes as a zip. Drag that zip back onto the page any time to carry on.
[/list]

The download is worth having for the 3D view with the game's own models, Game Pass saves, moving items between worlds, live editing and plugins.

[line]

[size=5][b]Plugins and languages[/b][/size]

The editor comes in English, German, Spanish, French and Russian. Language packs for other languages install as plugins.

Plugins can add bulk edits, fixes and new panels. A plugin can be a compiled .NET add-on or a plain JavaScript file. Only install plugins you trust: like any add-on, they run with full access to your computer.

[line]

[size=5][b]Staying up to date[/b][/size]

Download the newest file from the FILES tab and replace the old one. Your settings and plugins are kept. The editor shows what changed the first time you open a new version, and again from [b]Settings ▸ General ▸ SHOW RELEASE NOTES[/b]. Scoop users run [b]scoop update abiotic-editor[/b].

